          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Kjszwy5ddq23bSon1NzTAV",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining sparse lexical and dense vector search to improve retrieval quality in RAG systems.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-15T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/RAG\\n  - pattern/Hybrid-Search\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Multiple corroborating sources covering production implementation patterns.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\n**Hybrid retrieval** combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via [[reciprocal-rank-fusion]], to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\n### Performance Characteristics\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either method alone, particularly on queries mixing:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent\\n\\nThis makes it well-suited to production RAG systems where queries may contain both precise technical references and broader semantic intent.\\n\\n### Implementation\\n\\nThe typical pattern:\\n1. Execute a sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) against the corpus\\n2. Execute a dense vector search using semantic embeddings\\n3. Merge the ranked result sets using reciprocal rank fusion or another ranking strategy\\n4. Return the combined top-k results\\n\\n### Production Adoption\\n\\nHybrid retrieval has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems, replacing single-method approaches due to its superior performance on mixed-intent queries.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval evaluation in production RAG systems; reciprocal rank fusion as the primary merging strategy.\\n\u0022}"
